<h2>How the case unfolded</h2>
<p>The wife moved an application under Section 125 CrPC before the 9th Judicial Magistrate, Alipore (ACM 251 of 2022). In the affidavit of assets and liabilities that <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em> makes mandatory, she recorded her income as &ldquo;N/A&rdquo;. On 30 March 2024 the Magistrate directed interim maintenance of Rs 45,000 per month &mdash; an order the husband said was passed without hearing him. He carried the matter to the High Court in revision, seeking quashing of the proceeding and the order.</p>
<p>What undid the claim was the record itself. The wife&rsquo;s supplementary affidavit, read with a salary certificate from her employer, showed earnings higher than the husband&rsquo;s. Her explanation &mdash; that the original &ldquo;no income&rdquo; entry was a miscommunication with her advocate &mdash; did not persuade the court.</p>

<h2>The findings</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Deliberate suppression.</strong> The court held the wife had consciously concealed her income and misused the affidavit mechanism that exists precisely to put both parties&rsquo; finances on the table.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>An order passed in haste.</strong> The 30 March 2024 interim direction was made without hearing the parties or weighing the husband&rsquo;s objections — a process failure measured against the Rajnesh framework.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Abuse of process.</strong> With the wife earning more than the husband, the court concluded the proceeding was being pursued for personal vengeance rather than sustenance, and should not continue.</div>
</div>

<h2>The operative order</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Direction</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Proceeding quashed</td><td>The entire case (ACM 251 of 2022) before the 9th Judicial Magistrate, Alipore stands quashed</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Interim order quashed</td><td>The Rs 45,000/month order dated 30.03.2024 falls with it</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Costs</td><td>Rs 20,000 payable by the wife into the Juvenile Justice Fund (SLSA, West Bengal) within 10 days, receipt to the Registrar General</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The wider lesson for maintenance litigation</h2>
<p>Under the BNSS the provision is now Section 144, but the architecture is unchanged: interim maintenance is decided on competing sworn disclosures. Courts in Delhi routinely direct supplementary affidavits, summon employer records and draw adverse inferences from gaps. This judgment goes a step further than an adverse inference &mdash; it treats deliberate falsity as corroding the proceeding itself. Claimants should disclose fully, including income that may reduce the award; respondents facing inflated claims should pursue disclosure aggressively rather than merely denying liability. The affidavit is not a formality. It is the case.</p>
<div class="note">This report summarises a judgment of the Calcutta High Court dated 25 November 2026 for general information. It is not legal advice; entitlement to maintenance always turns on the financial record of the particular parties.</div>
